 IRC has operated in Nigeria since 2012, responding to the protracted humanitarian crisis in the Northeast and, more recently, growing needs in the Northwest. Programming addresses conflict, displacement, acute malnutrition, food insecurity and climate-related shocks, and spans food security, health, nutrition, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), protection and resilience. IRC Nigeria works closely with national and community-based organizations (CBOs) and has long-standing experience operating in the BAY states (Borno, Adamawa, Yobe) and an expanding footprint in the Northwest, including Zamfara and Katsina.
 FCDO is designing a new Nigeria humanitarian program, Responding to and Reducing Humanitarian Need through Investment in Systems & Resilience (RRISE), to replace the current Humanitarian Assistance and Resilience Program (HARP). RRISE is expected to be delivered through a Locally Led Response Intermediary Model (LLRIM), in which a lead organization - an INGO or consortium - holds the FCDO grant and works through national and local NGOs and CSOs to deliver the response directly to communities. FCDO RRISE remains at design and consultation stage. IRC's internal planning assumption for bid purposes is a complex program valued GBP 30 to 40 million over approximately 4 to 5 years; FCDO has confirmed that final budget and duration will be set through the Business Case and Call for Proposals process.
 SCOPE OF WORK 
 The IRC is seeking a Finance Lead for the upcoming FCDO Nigeria RRISE program. This program is expected to be delivered through a LLRIM, under which the IRC, as lead organization, would hold the FCDO Accountable Grant Agreement (AGA) and work through national and local NGOs, CBOs and CSOs to deliver food security, protection, nutrition, WASH, health and shelter assistance across the Northeast and Northwest of Nigeria, alongside a funded capacity strengthening component for national and local partners.
 Under the direct management of the Team Lead, the Finance Lead will lead the financial management for the lead organization under the AGA with FCDO. S/he will lead financial planning, budgeting, forecasting and cash management across the consortium, and oversee the design and administration of sub-grants to consortium and downstream national and local NGO and CSO partners. S/he will apply proportionate, risk-based due diligence, including due diligence passporting to avoid repeated and duplicative assessments, segmenting partners by role, geography, capability and risk profile in coordination with the Capacity Strengthening & Mentoring Senior Manager, so that funding instruments, financial oversight and support are proportionate to each partner’s capacity.
 The Finance Lead will build and lead the finance team within the lead organization, the size and structure of which will be confirmed once the program design and budget are finalized. S/he will work closely with the program team: Humanitarian Systems Strengthening Lead (on partner segmentation, sub-grant design, overhead recovery and strengthening partner financial systems), the MEAL Lead (on KPI and VfM metrics, cost tracking and benchmarking), the GEDSI and Safeguarding Lead (on safeguarding-sensitive due diligence and GEDSI), and consortium and downstream partners’ finance teams. S/he will represent the finance function in Program Management Unit (PMU) and donor reporting and will directly report to the Team Lead.
 Please note that recruitment is contingent upon the successful award of the project, and the selection of final applicants is subject to FCDO’s approval. 
