 Key Responsibilities: 
 The Warehouse Assistant will manage day-to-day operations at the Saida warehouse under World Vision Lebanon (WVL) management and accountability, while facilitating the safe and timely storage and dispatch of commodities supporting Nusaned distributions. The consultant will maintain accurate stock records, coordinate authorized requests and warehouse access, document receipts and dispatches, support post-distribution reconciliation, manage returned commodities, and promptly report discrepancies, losses or damages in line with agreed WVL procedures.
 Warehouse operations and shared-use coordination 
 Maintain the warehouse in an orderly, clean and secure condition;
 store commodities safely;
 Maintain clear segregation, labeling and stacking; ensure access is limited to authorized personnel; maintain an access log;
 Coordinate planned access and movements with WVL and Nusaned focal points. WVL retains overall management and accountability for the premises and stock.
 Receipt and inventory management 
 Check incoming commodities against delivery notes, purchase/donation records and quantities;
 Record batch/expiry details where applicable, storage location and condition; maintain updated stock cards/registers and electronic records;
 Conduct routine physical counts and apply first-expiry-first-out/first-in-first-out principles as appropriate.
 Commodity request and dispatch procedures 
 Receive requests through the agreed authorized focal points;
 Check that requests include item descriptions, quantities, intended distribution location/date and approval;
 Flag incomplete or unapproved requests. Requests should be submitted at least five working days before the planned distribution, unless an alternative timeline is agreed in writing.
 Prepare and verify commodities against approved pick lists and dispatch documents.
 Distribution documentation and reconciliation: 
 Maintain signed release/dispatch notes and proof of handover;
 Obtain distribution reports from the responsible distribution team after each cycle;
 Reconcile quantities dispatched, distributed, returned, damaged and remaining;
 Update stock balances and file all supporting records.
 Follow up promptly on missing reports or unexplained variances.
 Returned commodities: 
 Receive and count unused or returned items;
 Inspect and document their condition;
 Separate usable, damaged, expired or questionable items;
 Update inventory records and store returned items appropriately pending WVL instructions. Do not dispose of or redistribute items without authorization.
 Discrepancies, losses and damages: 
 Immediately secure affected stock and notify the designated WVL supervisor of any shortage, surplus, loss, damage, suspected misuse or documentation mismatch.
 Record details, preserve relevant documents and support stock counts/investigations.
 Do not adjust records or write off stock without written approval. Escalate urgent security or safety concerns immediately.
 Reporting and coordination: 
 Provide regular stock reports and movement summaries to WVL;
 Coordinate with Nusaned on upcoming distribution plans, quantities and collection schedules;
 Participate in stock counts and monitoring visits;
 Maintain organized filing of receipts, requests, dispatch notes, access logs and reconciliation records.
 Minimum Requirements: 
 Secondary education required; training in warehouse, logistics, inventory management or supply chain is an asset.
 Basic computer skills (Excel or inventory spreadsheets) required.
 Relevant warehouse or occupational health and safety training is an advantage.
 Previous experience in warehouse keeping, stock management, logistics, storekeeping or distribution support.
 Experience maintaining stock registers, checking delivery notes, preparing dispatches and conducting physical counts. Humanitarian or NGO experience is an asset.
 Arabic and English required.
 May require coordination with WVL and Nusaned teams and occasional travel to nearby distribution locations, subject to operational needs.
 Ability to stand and walk for extended periods, inspect and count stock, and handle or move commodities using safe manual-handling practices. Must follow safety procedures and seek assistance/equipment for heavy loads.
